# Linux cache cleaner

Clears regenerable caches left by package managers, desktop applications,
browsers, Flatpak, Snap, and temporary-file policies.

> **Preview first:** In the TUI, nothing is deleted until the preview finishes
> successfully and you type `CLEAN`. Apply repeats discovery with the same
> settings, so targets may change between the two runs.

## What it can clean

- APT, DNF, Yum, Zypper, Pacman, APK, XBPS, Portage, and RPM-OSTree caches
- XDG, Flatpak, Snap, and Firefox startup caches
- temporary data selected by installed user and system tmpfiles policies
- optional current-user entries in `/tmp` and `/var/tmp` with `--all-temp`
- optional archived systemd journals and unreachable Nix or Guix store objects

## Safety

Run it as your normal user. Commands that need elevated access use `sudo` or
`doas` individually. It does not target package databases, installed packages,
containers, volumes, kernel data, or broad system directories.

Package files used for offline reinstalls or downgrades may be removed. Close
applications before applying the plan. Run `sudo -v` before opening the TUI;
password prompts are intentionally disabled inside it.

## Options

| Option | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `--all-temp` | include recent current-user temp files |
| `--logs` | keep only seven days of archived systemd journals |
| `--store-gc` | collect unreachable Nix and Guix store objects |
| `--verbose` | print every filesystem target |
| `--apply` | repeat discovery with deletion enabled |
| `--yes` | accept the script's prompt after TUI confirmation |

## Direct CLI

```sh
sys/linux/clean --help
sys/linux/clean --verbose
sys/linux/clean --apply
```

## Exit status

`0` means the preview or cleanup completed without warnings. `1` means input,
platform, privilege, path validation, or one or more cleanup operations failed.
